00:05:20.025 --> 00:05:21.900
Why don't you tell us
from your perspective--

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:22.950
and then we'll come
back to you, Nenad--

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.306
about, like, what is Forseti?

00:05:24.306 --> 00:05:26.680
Like, at a high level, what
problem's it trying to solve?

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:27.690
What does it do?

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:28.523
ANDREW HOYING: Sure.

00:05:28.523 --> 00:05:33.570
So Forseti is about making
Cloud easier to secure at scale.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.000
It's relatively simple
for an individual

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.570
to secure one project, or
one environment on Cloud,

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.230
or 10 environments.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:46.090
But as you scale to 100 or 1,000
or even 10,000 environments,

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.090
becomes very difficult
for even a team of people

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:51.630
to maintain security across
all the disparate settings

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:54.390
and configurations that
we expose for Cloud.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.880
So Forseti is really about
enabling security teams

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:59.820
and enterprises to feel
comfortable and confident

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:03.420
that they're running all
of their different projects

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.880
in a way that meets
their security

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.050
policies and their security
configuration expectations.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:10.300
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: Then that--

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.030
on top of that,
I think it's also

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:15.960
about having the
ability to scale

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.070
towards an agile organization
and have those notifications

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:23.040
and make people are aware of
the misconfigurations they're

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.470
making in an actual
agile environment

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:28.780
so that they're on top of their
stuff as soon as it happens

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:30.480
or close to that time.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:33.730
MELANIE WARRICK: And so Nenad,
since you've used Forseti,

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:36.030
I think one question
that's coming to my mind

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:39.340
are what are some of the
common challenges that you

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:42.462
would have for dealing with
securing something at scale?

00:06:42.462 --> 00:06:44.670
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: So that
is a really good question.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:47.520
So to start with, I mean, yes.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.440
We've use Forseti, but we
also collaborated with Google

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.270
on actually making
Forseti what it is today.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:57.570
And of course, the goal
was in a modern environment

00:06:57.570 --> 00:07:01.110
with a huge amount of
servers, you actually

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.940
want to take care of the servers
and what is happening on them.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:07.470
And on top that, of course,
since it is the Cloud,

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.650
the Cloud also comes
with a bigger complexity

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:13.140
of having stuff on their metal
and in their local environment

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.570
because they end up
using multiple features

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:16.770
from that Cloud.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:20.380
So one is, yes, moving
to a new platform

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:21.870
and having that secured.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.030
And the second piece
is, of course, how

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:27.780
do you scale your security
with this [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.480
that you want to have
in an agile environment.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:33.340
So that is kind of how
we ended up using Forseti

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:36.360
and why we actually decided
that it is a good thing

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:38.370
to collaborate with Google.

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:41.420
Because we had one perspective
on what were missing

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:44.910
or what we wanted to have
as a tool, and then Google

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:46.050
had another perspective.

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:48.810
So having those
joined ends up us

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.820
having a good tool to do what
we need to do on the Cloud.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:54.180
MARK MANDEL: So that probably
segues quite nicely into,

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:54.820
like--

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:56.490
so what was the history here?

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:58.110
Where does this project start?

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.210
How did this
collaboration come to be?

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:01.043
ANDREW HOYING: Sure.

00:08:01.043 --> 00:08:02.490
I can take that
from Google's side

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:04.200
and then have Nenad fill in.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.325
Internally, we had started
using Google Cloud Platform here

00:08:07.325 --> 00:08:10.590
at Google, obviously, as soon
as we launched it as a product.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.590
And we had quite a large
deployment on Cloud,

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:16.560
even as the security
team was getting spun up.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.700
So we had a need to sort of
inventory everything that

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.300
was currently deployed on Cloud
and get sort of a baseline

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:25.800
configuration.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.680
And then as we've defined
security policies,

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:32.429
we needed a way to notify
different teams and project

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:34.919
owners that they needed
to modify a configuration

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:38.429
or make some setting
change in order to comply

00:08:38.429 --> 00:08:39.990
with our security policies.

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:44.580
So we needed some way to scale
very quickly up to a very, very

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:45.930
large environment.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:49.050
So we initially developed
some internal tooling

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:50.350
to allow us to do that.

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:53.040
But at the same time, we were
hearing from the customer

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:55.740
engineers in our professional
services organization

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:59.220
that customers were running
into this same issue

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:00.900
where they deployed
on Cloud but now

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:04.330
they need some way to monitor
and manage their environment.

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:08.430
So we collaborated--
security collaborated

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:10.740
with professional services
and a few other teams

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:13.290
within Google to
start putting together

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.820
this framework for
what became Forseti.

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:18.870
And at the same time, we worked
with our product managers

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:22.170
to look for some of the large
organizations who were already

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:25.410
on Google Cloud who might
want to collaborate on this.

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:27.884
And Spotify stepped
forward and--

00:09:27.884 --> 00:09:30.300
because they were building
similar tooling, as Nenad said.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:33.930
And it ended up being a
great working relationship

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:35.790
to have both an
outside perspective

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:39.300
and our inside perspective on
how to build a tool like this.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.577
MELANIE WARRICK: Does Forseti
come from Norse mythology?

00:09:42.577 --> 00:09:43.410
ANDREW HOYING: Yeah.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:47.650
So we went through a long naming
session, which was really--

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:49.620
was really rather entertaining.

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:52.000
And we had names from
all over the board.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:54.240
But one thing that
we liked about sort

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:56.700
of the concept of
Forseti is that this

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:00.510
is a overseer sort
of like trying

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:05.050
to maintain security
justice, if you will,

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:06.240
from the Norse mythology.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:10.260
So the name sort of just stuck
out from all the other ones

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:11.520
that we had from choices.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:13.860
And everybody ended up
agreeing that it was

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.690
a great name for this product.

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:17.094
So that's how it came about.

00:10:17.094 --> 00:10:18.510
MARK MANDEL: So
I'm quite curious.

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:21.580
You both also use this
term security at scale.

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:23.577
What does that actually mean?

00:10:23.577 --> 00:10:25.160
What does that, like
practically mean?

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:26.070
Like, what sort
of things are you

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:28.350
looking to do when you
do security at scale?

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:30.924
I don't know exactly
what that refers to.

00:10:30.924 --> 00:10:32.340
NENAD STOJANOVSKI:
So I think it's

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:35.250
kind of along the lines of
the whole equation on how

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:38.670
many security engineers do you
need per, I don't know, squads?

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:39.850
Teams?

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:42.840
And usually, you can't
have that many people.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:45.000
Even if you like it,
in the ideal world,

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:46.240
you won't have them.

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:47.980
And the same applies
for resources.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:51.600
It's like how many resources or
how many security engineers are

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:55.260
you going to put on
overseeing or checking

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:57.240
or monitoring resources?

00:10:57.240 --> 00:10:57.742
Right?

00:10:57.742 --> 00:10:58.700
So it is about scaling.

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:02.820
And it is about, I think,
giving autonomy to teams

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:05.400
and giving responsibility
to teams that build product

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:08.250
that they should be the
ones taking care of security

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:10.680
when it comes to
configuration and monitoring.

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:13.220
But at the same time, we
want to give them tools--

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:18.300
or want to give the organization
tools to scale to such size

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:21.600
that it is going to talk or it
is going to notify all teams

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:23.400
and help them
monitor their stuff.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:26.150
So that is kind of security
at scale and maybe security--

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:29.650
or self-service security
at the same time.

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:31.950
That is what it means to me.

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:34.230
ANDREW HOYING: Adding
onto that-- so one

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:37.680
of the great things about
Cloud is that it enables us

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:41.460
to segment different workloads
in different environments.

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:44.380
Sort of the evolution
if you will,

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:49.200
of computing from mainframes
to data centers to Cloud.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.230
As we move to Cloud,
we want to build

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:54.990
security sort of
in from the ground

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:58.140
up in a way that was difficult
to do in a data center, where

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:01.620
we can run each different
workload in its own environment

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:03.400
with its own security settings.

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:04.950
But as the number
of environments

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:09.580
you're managing scales up, you
can't scale up people to that.

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:11.800
You would quickly
run out of resources.

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:15.130
So we need tooling
to enable ourselves,

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:18.330
enterprises, to
run a vast number

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:22.080
of different environments
without needing 100 security

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.300
engineers to manage
them all, you know.

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.250
So that's really what
it's about-- scaling up

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:28.196
each security engineer
to be the most

00:12:28.196 --> 00:12:29.820
valuable, the most
productive that they

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:31.502
can be for the enterprise.

00:12:31.502 --> 00:12:33.210
MELANIE WARRICK: And
so Forseti's tooling

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:36.000
includes things like
inventory, scanner, enforcer,

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:36.885
and notifier.

00:12:36.885 --> 00:12:37.860
Is that right?

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:38.100
ANDREW HOYING: Yes.

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:38.730
That's correct.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:40.380
MELANIE WARRICK: And can you
explain a little bit more

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:41.710
about what those pieces do?

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:42.543
ANDREW HOYING: Sure.

00:12:42.543 --> 00:12:47.610
So inventory is simply
the crawler that says,

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:49.200
look at all of the
resources that are

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:50.810
running in your organization.

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:54.600
At Google Cloud Platform,
we have a resource hierarchy

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:58.440
that starts from an organization
node, which is everything that

00:12:58.440 --> 00:12:59.790
belongs to your organization.

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:02.070
Underneath that, you
can have folders.

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:03.852
Inside of those, you
can have projects.

00:13:03.852 --> 00:13:06.060
And inside of projects,
there are running resources--

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:09.030
your virtual instances,
your VMs, your storage,

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:10.500
and everything else.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:14.310
So Forseti takes and
crawls from the top down

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:17.400
to create a snapshot
inventory of everything that's

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.680
running in your
environment and stores

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:23.250
that in a Cloud SQL
storage currently.

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:24.810
The next bit is
the scanner, which

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:28.950
is a set of rules that audit
the configuration that all

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:29.640
those resources.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:33.030
So it might look at how your
Identity and Access Management

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:36.420
is configured to look for,
like, someone added from outside

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:39.840
of your organization that
shouldn't be as having access

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:44.790
to a project, or look at
your virtual instances

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:47.670
to see if they've got external
IP addresses where they

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:50.100
shouldn't, or look at
your firewall rules--

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:52.520
any sort of configuration
that exist on Cloud.

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:55.590
And we're constantly adding
additional capabilities

00:13:55.590 --> 00:13:57.330
to those scanners
so that you can

00:13:57.330 --> 00:13:59.610
look for all of the
common best practices

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:03.120
as well as put in place
your own custom requirements

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:06.940
for how you expect things to be
configured for your enterprise.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:09.960
Enforcer comes from
another internal tool

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:12.470
that we had called GC Enforcer.

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:17.280
And its purpose today is
to set firewall policy.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:20.177
So if you've got a
predefined configuration

00:14:20.177 --> 00:14:22.260
or set of configurations
that you want to push out

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:23.850
across all of your different--

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:26.020
or a subset of your
projects, enforcer

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:28.230
is the way to make sure
that those projects all

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:31.770
stay in sync with that
centrally managed configuration.

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:33.570
And then notifier
is the component

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:36.000
that takes any violations
from the scanners

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:40.020
as well as can be configured
to notify on some inventory

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:41.040
components as well.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:43.530
But primarily, it's
about taking a violation

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:46.140
and figuring out who
the project owner is

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:48.360
and sending a
notification saying,

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:51.480
your configuration is out
of sync, and please fix it--

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:54.570
again, pushing some
of the security

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:56.640
down to the individual
teams instead of

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:59.369
having security engineers have
to manage every environment.

00:14:59.369 --> 00:15:01.410
MARK MANDEL: This almost
sounds like the phrase--

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:03.450
I'm kind of thinking about
it as, like, as if it

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:04.950
was sort of a Terraform or--

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:05.170
ANDREW HOYING: Sure.

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:06.490
MARK MANDEL: --deployment
manager in Ansible,

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:08.240
or something like that
that's specifically

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:09.870
tailored to security.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:11.670
So you're defining
a source of truth

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:14.046
of what sort of firewall rules
or IM policies or anything

00:15:14.046 --> 00:15:16.461
like that of what should be,
and then it's up to this tool

00:15:16.461 --> 00:15:18.470
to make sure that that
is, like, the truth.

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:20.450
Do you feel like that's
a good summation?

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:21.283
ANDREW HOYING: Yeah.

00:15:21.283 --> 00:15:22.700
And, you know,
internally we work

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:25.610
with Terraform and Deployment
Manager for different projects

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:28.520
for maintaining their
specific security.

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:31.972
And for some subset of projects,
they're great tools for that

00:15:31.972 --> 00:15:33.680
because you have sort
of-- you've defined

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:37.940
your configuration as code
as some kind of predefined--

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:40.860
this is exactly how this
system should be set up.

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:43.327
But as Nenad said, in an
agile natural environment,

00:15:43.327 --> 00:15:45.410
you also have some projects
that are sort of being

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:48.590
developed on the fly or managed
by development teams or smaller

00:15:48.590 --> 00:15:49.640
squads.

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:53.400
And having a fully managed tool
isn't always best for them.

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:56.330
So having something that can
come through and sort of audit

00:15:56.330 --> 00:15:59.230
the configuration
post-deployment

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:00.302
is also really powerful.

00:16:00.302 --> 00:16:02.510
And being able to do that
across all of your projects

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:04.940
is really the key to what
Forseti brings to the table.

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:05.940
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: Yeah.

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:07.550
And if I may add to that--

00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:09.720
so there's also
the Enforcer piece,

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:13.130
and that is about opening up
your perimeter to the world.

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:17.010
So that one is about having
a centrally located database,

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:19.430
which is the source of truth
or firewall rules, which

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:22.730
is enforced so that it prevents
people from making mistakes.

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:24.020
That is the other piece.

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:27.150
And I think there were some
discussions about maybe

00:16:27.150 --> 00:16:28.790
adding something for Buckets.

00:16:28.790 --> 00:16:32.376
But then, it is maybe something
that is upcoming as a feature.

00:16:32.376 --> 00:16:33.250
ANDREW HOYING: Right.

00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:33.600
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:36.020
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: But it is
about tying the loose ends.

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:36.740
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:36.970
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:38.420
So this sounds
really interesting.

00:16:38.420 --> 00:16:41.900
If people, like, want to use or
configure or install Forseti,

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:43.400
like, how do we go
about doing that?

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:44.950
Is it, like-- is there
a push button installed?

00:16:44.950 --> 00:16:46.158
Or I download it from GitHub?

00:16:46.158 --> 00:16:46.890
What do I do?

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:47.723
ANDREW HOYING: Sure.

00:16:47.723 --> 00:16:50.050
So we've got a
Deployment Wizard that's

00:16:50.050 --> 00:16:53.060
on GitHub that sits
alongside of Forseti.

00:16:53.060 --> 00:16:56.690
So on the website
forsetisecurity.org,

00:16:56.690 --> 00:16:59.660
there is a number of
resources, including

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:01.970
the quickstart
installation guide.

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:04.550
But the really high
level is you start

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:07.579
a Cloud Shell on
a project that you

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:09.410
want to install Forseti into.

00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:11.750
You run the Deployment Wizard
from within that shell,

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:13.579
and it walks you
through everything

00:17:13.579 --> 00:17:14.919
to get your environment set up.

00:17:14.919 --> 00:17:16.460
It puts all of the
resources in place

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:19.579
that Forseti needs
and spins up the VM

00:17:19.579 --> 00:17:22.200
and then gives you
access to start using it.

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:23.817
So we've tried to
make the install as

00:17:23.817 --> 00:17:26.150
straightforward as possible
so that you can get straight

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:28.024
to configuring and
using the data.

00:17:28.024 --> 00:17:29.690
MARK MANDEL: Am I
configuring this with,

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:33.069
like, YAML files, or is there
a GUI, or how does that work?

00:17:33.069 --> 00:17:33.860
ANDREW HOYING: Yes.

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:37.640
It's all configured with YAML
files stored in Google Cloud

00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:38.930
Storage buckets.

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:40.730
So each of the
different scanners

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:44.390
has their own rule file
that defines your policy

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:47.900
for that particular resource
along with an overall

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:51.290
configuration file that sort of
defines what resources you're

00:17:51.290 --> 00:17:54.290
reading into the inventory,
and what your organization is,

00:17:54.290 --> 00:17:56.249
and what notifiers you
have enabled, et cetera.

00:17:56.249 --> 00:17:57.456
MELANIE WARRICK: Well anyway.

00:17:57.456 --> 00:17:59.450
So in terms of the
way it's being used,

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:01.307
would you also use
this in development?

00:18:01.307 --> 00:18:02.390
ANDREW HOYING: Absolutely.

00:18:02.390 --> 00:18:06.860
So this is a great way for
just getting a handle on--

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:09.980
if you're spinning up some
new resources in the Cloud,

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:12.620
or you're trying to
develop how you're

00:18:12.620 --> 00:18:16.190
going to ultimately deploy
your resources in the Cloud,

00:18:16.190 --> 00:18:18.410
having Forseti already up
and running and getting

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:21.200
it configured in a
development environment

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:22.790
prior to rolling it
out to production

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:25.730
is really powerful because
it can give you insights

00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:27.080
to misconfigurations.

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:29.797
You know, everybody tries
to do the right thing.

00:18:29.797 --> 00:18:31.380
But sometimes, when
we're moving fast,

00:18:31.380 --> 00:18:33.650
we accidentally
leave some system

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:35.990
open to the internet,
or some other exposure

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:37.490
that we didn't mean to.

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:39.707
So having Forseti
there to notify,

00:18:39.707 --> 00:18:41.540
hey, you've got this
configuration in place.

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:42.830
Did you mean to?

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:46.850
Is very powerful, even if it's
just a development environment.

00:18:46.850 --> 00:18:49.670
You don't want to have
a security incident

00:18:49.670 --> 00:18:50.940
anywhere in your organization.

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:51.690
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:53.480
MELANIE WARRICK: Sometimes those
are the things you might not

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:54.460
necessarily consider--

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:54.900
ANDREW HOYING: Right.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:56.160
MELANIE WARRICK:
--that can bite you.

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:56.312
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: Yeah.

00:18:56.312 --> 00:18:58.190
And I mean, to add
to that, I mean

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:00.940
it's usually about taking a
very holistic approach, which

00:19:00.940 --> 00:19:01.460
is yeah.

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:03.440
So you actually don't
know what you're

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:05.360
exposing in the dev
environment or how

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:06.740
it's connected to production.

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:07.790
Maybe it's not.

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:09.830
But in general, you
don't know how it's

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:11.120
connected to your [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:13.580
So it's better to
be safe than sorry.

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:17.520
So you just focus of use Forseti
wherever you need to use it.

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:20.170
But the holistic approach
would be the best,

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:21.680
at least from my perspective.

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:23.040
And that is my advice.

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:23.680
And yeah.

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:25.000
You just go from there.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:26.200
So once-- one--

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:29.240
I mean, you can actually test
it out in the dev environment

00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:32.780
and how it scales up and how
you will get notifications

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:34.690
once it goes into production.

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:36.860
And then the second
one is, yes, you also

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:39.140
get to learn about
misconfiguration.

00:19:39.140 --> 00:19:41.950
So you can either
automate them if you're

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:45.260
deploying too many instances or
in general too many resources.

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:48.030
And at the same time, you
know what the problem is.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:49.700
So sometimes, it's
not very obvious,

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:51.262
but you get to learn from it.

00:19:51.262 --> 00:19:53.720
MARK MANDEL: And this sounds
like something you'd want to--

00:19:53.720 --> 00:19:55.470
in an ideal world,
you'd want to slot this

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:56.930
in as early as possible, right?

00:19:56.930 --> 00:19:59.180
You don't want to be making
changes to your production

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:00.116
system in production.

00:20:00.116 --> 00:20:01.490
You don't know
necessarily what's

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:03.020
going to happen-- if
it's going to lock down

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:04.820
certain ports, which will stop
certain things from talking

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:06.320
to each other or
that kind of stuff.

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:07.160
Would you agree?

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:07.370
ANDREW HOYING: Yes.

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:07.870
Absolutely.

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:10.100
The earlier you can
deploy it and start

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:11.630
getting the
visibility, the easier

00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:13.130
it's going to be
to make sure you've

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:17.170
got all your best practices
in place from the beginning.

00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:19.660
And when you go to
production, you're confident.

00:20:19.660 --> 00:20:20.710
MELANIE WARRICK: Is there
anything in particular

00:20:20.710 --> 00:20:22.750
you've seen people miss
when you-- you know,

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:25.510
we're talking about trying
to have a holistic approach--

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:29.000
the thing that, you know, seems
obvious or seems so simple,

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:32.544
but yet has proved to be
one of the bigger gaps?

00:20:32.544 --> 00:20:33.710
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: We make--

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:34.210
yeah.

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:36.970
I can go from my perspective
and my learnings.

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:39.850
And that is sometimes
it is hard for people

00:20:39.850 --> 00:20:44.820
to realize how different
GCP is from some competitors

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:46.660
without name dropping.

00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:47.710
It's not cool.

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:48.840
So yeah.

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:52.960
Sometimes, something that
competitor has as an option

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:54.860
might be something
different than GCP.

00:20:54.860 --> 00:20:57.880
So it is a big
misunderstanding to start with.

00:20:57.880 --> 00:21:02.140
And then of course, the logic
and the business logic behind--

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:05.350
well, not only business
logic, but also tech logic--

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:08.500
behind GCP is totally
different from something else

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:09.960
that might be out there.

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:12.850
So most of those
happen because of that.

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:14.880
And then, of course,
having the best practices

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:18.100
on how things work is probably
very helpful for people.

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:19.600
MELANIE WARRICK: I
was going to say,

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:21.433
without name dropping
or necessarily talking

00:21:21.433 --> 00:21:24.760
about other setups, is there
anything specific in GCP

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:27.350
that you're like, yeah, that
was very different for us

00:21:27.350 --> 00:21:29.350
and stood out,
that comes to mind?

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:31.600
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: One
thing that comes to mind

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:34.110
would be maybe how
buckets work or--

00:21:34.110 --> 00:21:35.460
MELANIE WARRICK: Ah the buckets.

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:36.525
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: GCS storage.

00:21:36.525 --> 00:21:37.170
Yeah.

00:21:37.170 --> 00:21:39.400
So because yes, of course.

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:42.010
You have the IM policy,
and all of a sudden,

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:45.220
it becomes a bit confusing
because maybe GCS buckets

00:21:45.220 --> 00:21:47.570
don't follow the IM
model to start with.

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:48.640
And then yes.

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:51.160
So maybe the docs
are a bit confusing.

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:52.600
What is an authenticated user?

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:55.660
What is an org
user to start with?

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:57.640
And some of those
misconfigurations

00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:02.747
happen there because it
is how users are treated.

00:22:02.747 --> 00:22:03.580
MELANIE WARRICK: OK.

00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:06.249
So Forseti was able to
help with addressing that.

00:22:06.249 --> 00:22:08.290
MARK MANDEL: It sounds
like Forseti kind of takes

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:09.550
the assumption away, right?

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:12.114
It's like, you're very specific
about exactly how things

00:22:12.114 --> 00:22:13.780
are, rather than just
assuming something

00:22:13.780 --> 00:22:15.000
works in a certain way.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:15.980
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: Yes.

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:16.850
That is correct.

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:19.414
But then, of course, it
is, again, up to the user

00:22:19.414 --> 00:22:20.830
to actually decide
on how they are

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:22.250
going to define
the rules, right,

00:22:22.250 --> 00:22:23.920
and what is acceptable to them.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:26.290
There are some best
practices rules, but yes.

00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:29.350
So it depends on how
they use the resources

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:32.410
and how they approach the
openness of the environment--

00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:35.590
or their openness to the world.

00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:37.630
ANDREW HOYING: I would
add to that in general,

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:40.252
access control policies
are difficult for someone

00:22:40.252 --> 00:22:42.460
who is not constantly working
with them as a security

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:43.380
engineer.

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:45.550
You usually just
want to get things up

00:22:45.550 --> 00:22:47.100
and running and working.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:51.880
And so it's easy to
accidentally over-grant access

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:54.077
because you can't tell
necessarily that you've

00:22:54.077 --> 00:22:54.910
over-granted access.

00:22:54.910 --> 00:22:56.530
You can just tell
that it's working.

00:22:56.530 --> 00:22:58.660
So having something
follow up and look

00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:01.000
at what the actual configured
policy is against what

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:03.340
the expectation is
and notify people

00:23:03.340 --> 00:23:07.160
when they have granted too
much access or over-exposed

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:10.210
a resource is really powerful,
both for the learning

00:23:10.210 --> 00:23:12.880
of the individual
system owner, as well as

00:23:12.880 --> 00:23:15.250
for maintaining the
overall security

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:17.334
of all of the environment.

00:23:17.334 --> 00:23:18.250
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:23:18.250 --> 00:23:20.499
I've seen that be a common
problem before, too.

00:23:20.499 --> 00:23:21.790
It's just like, I can't get in.

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:23.510
Let's just give
access to everything.

00:23:23.510 --> 00:23:26.170
And it's clearly the
wrong way to go, but yeah.

00:23:26.170 --> 00:23:26.894
It's a hard one.

00:23:26.894 --> 00:23:29.185
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: And I
mean, without overcomplicating

00:23:29.185 --> 00:23:33.580
the answer, but it's also about
an internal security culture,

00:23:33.580 --> 00:23:37.300
and how open the culture is,
and whether people come and talk

00:23:37.300 --> 00:23:39.730
to the security team or
they avoid it and try

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:41.100
to find solutions.

00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:44.290
And that is where maybe hacks
happen, where people just

00:23:44.290 --> 00:23:45.940
try to solve their problem--

00:23:45.940 --> 00:23:48.520
maybe not the right way
of solving the problem,

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:50.240
but it is about
solving a problem.

00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:50.790
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:51.540
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:52.660
We've talked about
it a little bit,

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:55.630
but I'd love to hear more about,
like, what happens when Forseti

00:23:55.630 --> 00:23:59.050
finds something that doesn't
comply with the policy that's

00:23:59.050 --> 00:23:59.890
been set up.

00:23:59.890 --> 00:24:01.220
It sounds like there's
kind of two options.

00:24:01.220 --> 00:24:03.053
There's an automated
fix and a notification.

00:24:03.053 --> 00:24:04.990
Could you talk a little
bit more about those?

00:24:04.990 --> 00:24:06.400
I know you're using
this a lot at Spotify.

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:08.380
Why don't you talk about
how that works at your end?

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:10.540
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: I can
talk about the automated

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:11.680
notification.

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:14.140
So the automated
notification is depending

00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:16.280
on what you prefer to use.

00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:18.880
You have several ways of
getting notified as a team.

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:22.090
So that can be emails,
Slack, whatever you prefer.

00:24:22.090 --> 00:24:24.460
And of course, depending on
what policies you've enabled

00:24:24.460 --> 00:24:27.020
and what the rules are
for those policies,

00:24:27.020 --> 00:24:29.710
they will generate violations
on the Forseti backend.

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:31.740
Then as Andrew
mentioned, there's

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:35.620
the notifier tool, which
takes those violations

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:38.470
and they adjust
them-- or ingest them

00:24:38.470 --> 00:24:41.770
to create an actual
format of a notification,

00:24:41.770 --> 00:24:44.140
either for slack or email.

00:24:44.140 --> 00:24:46.750
And then it also finds
the owner at the same time

00:24:46.750 --> 00:24:48.880
and notifies the
owner, which is yes.

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:52.770
So for example, you own this
project, we found a violation,

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:54.520
let's say in your GCS storage.

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:57.985
And this is the actual issue,
and this is how you fix it.

00:24:57.985 --> 00:25:00.175
So everything is
kind of configurable

00:25:00.175 --> 00:25:02.340
on what kind of a
message you send.

00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:05.590
And when it comes to the
automated remediation,

00:25:05.590 --> 00:25:08.200
that is only on the
Enforcer side of things,

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:10.120
and it is only for
firewall rules.

00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:12.640
So if you, by mistake,
open something

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:14.950
and then you have
Enforcer enabled,

00:25:14.950 --> 00:25:18.230
it will reapply the rules
based on the centrally stored

00:25:18.230 --> 00:25:20.330
database or centrally stored.

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:22.490
So those are the two
modes of operation.

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:25.090
MELANIE WARRICK: Can you tell
us about what's coming out?

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:25.923
ANDREW HOYING: Yeah.

00:25:25.923 --> 00:25:27.700
And this is really a
great segue to some

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:30.460
of the new things that's
coming out here in the coming

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:31.755
months for Forseti.

00:25:31.755 --> 00:25:33.880
There's going to be a new
version of Forseti that's

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:36.865
released here at the end
of March, early April,

00:25:36.865 --> 00:25:38.740
which might already be
out by the time you're

00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:41.470
listening to this, which
is the Forseti 2.0.

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:45.340
It's got a totally
redesigned inventory system

00:25:45.340 --> 00:25:47.750
that crawls resources
much faster.

00:25:47.750 --> 00:25:51.315
So it scales to a much larger
organization than 1.0 can,

00:25:51.315 --> 00:25:52.690
or at least it
allows you to take

00:25:52.690 --> 00:25:55.510
a snapshot of your inventory
at more frequent intervals.

00:25:55.510 --> 00:25:59.830
And it's got a new
component in addition to

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:01.720
the four we've already
talked about called

00:26:01.720 --> 00:26:05.660
IM Explainer, which allows
you to ask questions like,

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:09.790
who in my organization can
restart this particular VM.

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:11.890
And it will crawl all
of the IM Settings

00:26:11.890 --> 00:26:15.940
across the organization
and resolve those

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:19.720
to a set of permissions and
then answer this set of people

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:23.140
can perform that action or
any other type of question

00:26:23.140 --> 00:26:26.630
that you might have like
who can delete a resource,

00:26:26.630 --> 00:26:30.250
or who all has access to
this database, et cetera.

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:32.590
So that's a very
powerful tool, especially

00:26:32.590 --> 00:26:34.660
in complex organizations.

00:26:34.660 --> 00:26:36.130
Without getting
too deep into it,

00:26:36.130 --> 00:26:39.860
identity and access management
policies are hierarchical.

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:43.540
So policies can be set on the
organization, or on a folder,

00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:46.420
or on a project,
or on a resource.

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:48.410
And the combination
of all of those

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:50.260
ends up being the total
set of policies that

00:26:50.260 --> 00:26:52.010
are applied to that resource.

00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:54.820
So it can be difficult to reason
about it without a tool like IM

00:26:54.820 --> 00:26:55.900
Explainer.

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:58.060
In addition to that,
after 2.0 launches,

00:26:58.060 --> 00:27:02.500
we've got a new set of
auditors and a new tool

00:27:02.500 --> 00:27:04.120
called Action Engine,
which is going

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:06.830
to be a combination of
Enforcer and Notifier

00:27:06.830 --> 00:27:08.140
into the single tooling.

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:11.740
And that Action Engine will
allow a much greater set

00:27:11.740 --> 00:27:14.200
of resources to be
automatically remediated

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:15.500
than just firewall rules.

00:27:15.500 --> 00:27:17.410
So we're very
excited about that.

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:20.230
That's most likely more
towards the end of June

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:22.360
before we're going to
see that in the product.

00:27:22.360 --> 00:27:24.760
But that's sort of
the two big things

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:27.190
that we're working on
right now that we're really

00:27:27.190 --> 00:27:29.540
excited to get out and
in front of people.

00:27:29.540 --> 00:27:32.620
The 2.0 release candidate
one is out today,

00:27:32.620 --> 00:27:34.210
so you can go ahead
and try it out.

00:27:34.210 --> 00:27:36.340
And check on the
website, because 2.0

00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:37.750
should be released here soon.

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:38.708
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:27:38.708 --> 00:27:41.640
And so if others wanted
to contribute to Forseti,

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:43.900
is there a place that
they can contribute?

00:27:43.900 --> 00:27:47.320
ANDREW HOYING: Yes we very
much value the community,

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:50.170
and we've got contributions
from many, many different

00:27:50.170 --> 00:27:53.260
organizations, many different
open-source individuals

00:27:53.260 --> 00:27:53.990
already.

00:27:53.990 --> 00:27:58.550
So our GitHub is, of course, the
source of truth for our code.

00:27:58.550 --> 00:28:01.730
And forsetisecurity.org
has a good page

00:28:01.730 --> 00:28:05.660
on contributing and becoming
a contributor to Forseti.

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:08.821
And we're always available
for taking questions.

00:28:08.821 --> 00:28:10.570
There's a mailing list
off of that website

00:28:10.570 --> 00:28:12.040
as well where you can email.

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:15.890
And we'd be very glad to work
with you on getting set up

00:28:15.890 --> 00:28:17.646
to contribute back to Forseti.

00:28:17.646 --> 00:28:18.520
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:28:18.520 --> 00:28:19.660
We're almost
running out of time,

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:20.993
but it's been super, super cool.

00:28:20.993 --> 00:28:23.170
Before we finish,
do either of you

00:28:23.170 --> 00:28:24.550
have anything you want to plug--

00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:26.560
events, stuff that's
going on, places

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:27.834
people can go to learn more?

00:28:27.834 --> 00:28:29.500
What's cool and
interesting with Forseti

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:31.430
that people should know
about before we finish?

00:28:31.430 --> 00:28:32.430
NENAD STOJANOVSKI: Yeah.

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:34.850
So, as Andrew mentioned,
forsetisecurity.org--

00:28:34.850 --> 00:28:36.915
place to go and
check things out.

00:28:36.915 --> 00:28:39.700
As me being a tech
nerd, I would definitely

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:42.430
go to GitHub or the
GitHub page because there

00:28:42.430 --> 00:28:44.216
are a lot of things out there.

00:28:44.216 --> 00:28:46.520
Yeah. and I think those
are the resources.

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:49.180
And to add to that, if
you find it interesting,

00:28:49.180 --> 00:28:51.326
contribute because it is
a really cool project.

00:28:51.326 --> 00:28:52.200
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:53.440
And Andrew, anything to add?

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:54.190
ANDREW HOYING: No.

00:28:54.190 --> 00:28:55.430
I think that pretty
much wraps it up.

00:28:55.430 --> 00:28:56.971
There's a lot of
exciting things that

00:28:56.971 --> 00:28:59.050
are going to be coming
out here this year.

00:28:59.050 --> 00:29:02.540
So please stay tuned.

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:04.510
We're excited about where
the product's going.

00:29:04.510 --> 00:29:06.010
And we want to hear
feedback, and we

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:08.710
want to hear your requirements
and what you would think

00:29:08.710 --> 00:29:09.850
would make it better.

00:29:09.850 --> 00:29:10.808
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:29:10.808 --> 00:29:12.305
Well, Nenad,
Andrew, thank you so

00:29:12.305 --> 00:29:14.805
