00:29:36.610 --> 00:29:38.690
Last thing is the
question of the week.

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:41.350
How would I automatically
scan the Docker images

00:29:41.350 --> 00:29:44.465
in my Google Cloud repository
for known vulnerabilities?

00:29:44.465 --> 00:29:45.340
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:29:45.340 --> 00:29:47.260
So this is a really
useful thing.

00:29:47.260 --> 00:29:50.490
I am going to totally
massacre my teammate's name.

00:29:50.490 --> 00:29:52.750
Fellow developer
advocate David--

00:29:52.750 --> 00:29:54.430
I'm going to go with [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:55.390
MELANIE WARRICK: I think
we need at some point

00:29:55.390 --> 00:29:57.760
to do, like, just a
mix of all of the names

00:29:57.760 --> 00:29:59.370
that you've tried to say.

00:29:59.370 --> 00:30:01.870
MARK MANDEL: David, I'm sorry
when I meet you in person soon

00:30:01.870 --> 00:30:02.661
in the near future.

00:30:02.661 --> 00:30:03.340
I apologize.

00:30:03.340 --> 00:30:05.170
But anyway, David
writes an excellent--

00:30:05.170 --> 00:30:07.820
most excellent blog
post talking about this.

00:30:07.820 --> 00:30:10.084
So basically, the
scenario is that if you're

00:30:10.084 --> 00:30:12.250
creating Docker images for
something like Kubernetes

00:30:12.250 --> 00:30:14.710
or several other things that
could be potentially possible,

00:30:14.710 --> 00:30:16.857
having a way to
automatically scan

00:30:16.857 --> 00:30:18.690
those for potential
security vulnerabilities

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:19.830
is really, really useful.

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:22.020
What's great is that
Google Cloud Platform

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:24.540
has a Container Registry
vulnerability scanning service

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:26.190
that's currently
in alpha that is

00:30:26.190 --> 00:30:28.020
really easy to turn on and use.

00:30:28.020 --> 00:30:28.980
It's basically an API.

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:31.950
You can go into your API manager
and basically just turn it on.

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:34.800
